1
00:00:00,990 --> 00:00:03,670
The International Space
Station is falling right

2
00:00:03,670 --> 00:00:04,470
now.

3
00:00:04,630 --> 00:00:06,930
It has been for
more than 25 years.

4
00:00:07,830 --> 00:00:09,130
So why doesn't it
hit the ground?

5
00:00:09,970 --> 00:00:13,310
Up there, about 400
kilometres above us, gravity

6
00:00:13,310 --> 00:00:16,590
is still about 90% as
strong as it is down here.

7
00:00:17,110 --> 00:00:19,390
If the station just stopped,
it would come straight

8
00:00:19,390 --> 00:00:20,190
down.

9
00:00:20,370 --> 00:00:22,450
The trick is that it's
also moving sideways.

10
00:00:23,210 --> 00:00:24,010
Very fast.

11
00:00:24,910 --> 00:00:27,250
About 8 kilometres every second.

12
00:00:27,250 --> 00:00:29,050
Picture throwing a ball.

13
00:00:29,550 --> 00:00:31,410
It curves down and lands.

14
00:00:32,370 --> 00:00:34,770
Throw it harder, and
it lands further away.

15
00:00:35,550 --> 00:00:38,110
Throw it hard enough, and
the ground curves away

16
00:00:38,110 --> 00:00:40,310
beneath it as fast as it falls.

17
00:00:41,190 --> 00:00:43,270
It keeps falling,
and it keeps missing.

18
00:00:44,010 --> 00:00:44,810
That's an orbit.

19
00:00:45,250 --> 00:00:47,430
The station goes all the
way around the Earth about

20
00:00:47,430 --> 00:00:50,110
every 90 minutes,
16 times a day.

21
00:00:50,910 --> 00:00:53,430
And the astronauts float,
because they're falling

22
00:00:53,430 --> 00:00:57,490
too, at exactly the same
rate as the station around

23
00:00:57,490 --> 00:00:58,290
them.

24
00:00:58,550 --> 00:01:01,210
Even so, at that height,
there's still a tiny bit

25
00:01:01,210 --> 00:01:04,290
of air, and it slowly
drags the station lower.

26
00:01:04,970 --> 00:01:07,650
So every now and then,
an engine gives it a push

27
00:01:07,650 --> 00:01:08,810
to climb back up.

28
00:01:09,270 --> 00:01:13,210
Around 2030, NASA plans
to stop pushing and steer

29
00:01:13,210 --> 00:01:15,190
it down into a remote
part of the ocean.
